The generated sizes are the powers of two from 2 to 1,024
(from 4 for `sm-two-cycles`, `sm-two-cycles-n-tokens`, `mg-buffer-ring` and `mg-buffer-ring-unmarked-circuit`).
The largest parametrized net used in the evaluation (`sm-choice-ring` with $n = 1,024$) has
3,072 places and 4,096 transitions.

*MCC models.* From the model repository of the 2026 Model Checking Contest #footnote[https://mcc.lip6.fr/models.php]
I drew 90 "small" and 20 "large" non-colored Petri nets by a fixed rule
(for the small models, the model nearest the median state-space size of its structural class, at most two per class;
for the large models, five per examination, one per class),
where a small net is one whose state space is known to be at most $10^6$ markings,
and a large net is one whose state space is larger than that or unknown.

MCC models which `petrivet` failed to parse, or which exceeded a file-size cap, were skipped:
#val("e0.mcc.small.rejected_not_connected") small and #val("e0.mcc.large.rejected_not_connected") large non-connected nets,
#val("e0.mcc.small.rejected_arc_weight") small and #val("e0.mcc.large.rejected_arc_weight") large nets which contained nonstandard ($!= 1$) arc weights, and
#val("e0.mcc.large.rejected_too_large") large nets whose file size exceeded 5 MB.

@fig-eval-mcc-small and @fig-eval-mcc-large show examples of a small and a large MCC model, respectively.

